--TEST--
`echo` inside an HTTP request escapes a string and writes a `Core\Html\Markup` as it is, where the same string echoed off a request comes back as it was written — `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape`, `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink`'s table in one run. Row one is an HTTP
// request, whose sink is the response body and whose carrier is
// `Core\Html\Markup`; row two is this program, whose sink is the terminal and
// whose carrier is `Cli\Text`. The same bytes go into both, so what is asserted
// is the **sink** deciding the transform — a value carrying a mark of its own
// would answer the same in either.
//
// The child is the `catch`, as in every in-process request case: a request made
// from inside one is refused, so the block handling that refusal is the handler
// the request ran.
try {
    var $answered = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/echoed");
    // A bound named on both sides, and the two operands differ only in type:
    // the string is escaped and the carrier spelling the same bytes is written
    // as it is. A sink that escaped everything would lose the markup and one
    // that escaped nothing would hand back the string, and either failure
    // prints plausibly against one half alone.
    echo "request: ", $answered->body(), "\n";

    // Row two, over the operand row one escaped: the string is written as it
    // was, because the escape belongs to the request's sink and never to the
    // value.
    echo "program: ", "<b>bold</b>", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "<b>bold</b>";
    echo " ";
    echo "<b>bold</b>" as Core\Html\Markup;
}
--EXPECT--
request: &lt;b&gt;bold&lt;/b&gt; <b>bold</b>
program: <b>bold</b>
